&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;This roundtable is open only to currently enrolled Columbia University students. To register, you must sign in with your UNI.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Center on Global Energy Policy (CGEP) at Columbia SIPA and the Fashion, Energy, and Climate Network invite you to join Session 2 of our &amp;ldquo;Fashion in the Energy Transition&amp;rdquo; talk series on Trade and Fast Fashion.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Global trade has shaped the fashion industry for decades, from the rise of international textile trade to today&amp;rsquo;s highly concentrated fast fashion supply chains. In this session, network founder Sally Qiu will speak with &lt;a href="https://www.energypolicy.columbia.edu/trevor-sutton/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Trevor Sutton&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, Director of the Program on Trade and the Clean Energy Transition at CGEP, to explore why trade and trade policy are increasingly central to fashion&amp;rsquo;s decarbonization.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We will discuss how trade dynamics influence where and how fashion is produced, affecting embedded emissions through local power systems, industrial energy use, and logistics. We&amp;rsquo;ll also examine how tariffs, standards, and other trade measures can reshape fast fashion and overproduction, shift sourcing and investment, and either accelerate or hinder access to cleaner energy and lower-carbon production. The conversation will also touch on how trade policy intersects with circular economy strategies, as well as the links between trade and agriculture-based fiber and their sustainability implications. Finally, we will examine the trade dimensions of fashion waste and its uneven impacts across the Global South.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About the series:&lt;/strong&gt; Fashion in the Energy Transition talk series connects the dots between the fashion value chain and the global energy transition, highlighting fashion&amp;rsquo;s often overlooked role in climate and energy conversations through interactive discussions with CGEP experts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Via Zoom,&lt;strong&gt; IN SPANISH&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Presentation of the volume &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Translation, Travel, Exchange: Microhistory in Ibero-America.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; special issue of&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Romanic Review&lt;/em&gt;, 116, 2, 2025, edited by&amp;nbsp;Rafael Gaune&amp;nbsp;Corradi and Nicol&amp;aacute;s Kwiatkowski.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The volume brings together contributions from a workshop sponsored by Romanic Review in 2023. The papers seek to discuss the presence of microhistory, both in the theoretical and methodological development of its areas of interest and in its application to specific studies in Ibero-America. Also, the articles explore the limits, the fragilities, the absences, as well as the possible end of microhistory&amp;rsquo;s momentum in Ibero-America.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
Speakers:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cristiana Schettini&lt;/strong&gt; (COCINET, and National University of San Mart&amp;iacute;n, Argentina)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Max Hering Torres&lt;/strong&gt; (National University of Colombia, Bogot&amp;aacute;)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Discussants:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Nicol&amp;aacute;s Kwiatkowski&lt;/strong&gt; (UPF, Barcelona - CONICET/UNSAM, Argentina).&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rafael Gaune&amp;nbsp;Corradi&lt;/strong&gt; (University of Padua - Catholic University of Chile).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Moderator:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pier Mattia Tommasino&lt;/strong&gt; (Columbia University)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This event is co-sponsored by the Department of Italian, the European Institute, the Hispanic Institute, and the Department of Latin American and Iberian Cultures at Columbia University, and by the Pontificia Universidad Cat&amp;oacute;lica de Chile, Universit&amp;agrave; di Padova, and Universitat Pompeu Fabra, Barcelona.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Artificial Intelligence is reshaping procedures and driving decision-making at lending institutions. This panel examines how risk leaders should structure AI risk governance at banks, private lenders, and government lending institutions. Panelists will discuss the distinctions between predictive, generative, and agentic AI, and they will explore the novel risks that generative AI introduces along with practical steps to mitigate those risks and responsibly adopt AI in alignment with risk appetite.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Attendees will leave with practical guidance on:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Top‑down governance building blocks including strategy, rules of the road, risk framework, assessment, and organizational reinforcement&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Bottom-up risk-tiering and controls for specific use cases&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Third-party/model risk considerations and resources required to sustain governance at scale&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Most common and impactful AI use cases in loan underwriting and risk management&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Moderator:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Deniz Ozenbas,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;Part-Time Lecturer, Enterprise Risk Management&lt;/p&gt;
