&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A Forum on University Governance&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In March 2026, the University Senate will host&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;A Forum on University Governance&lt;/strong&gt;. The Forum will first be held on the Morningside Campus and will be repeated at the Columbia University Irving Medical Center. The forum will explore:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What Would It Mean to &amp;ldquo;Democratize&amp;rdquo; a University? David Pozen, Charles Keller Beekman Professor of Law, Columbia Law School&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Democratizing Governance at Columbia; Starting from the Top: Jean Howard, George Delacorte Professor Emerita in the Humanities and Member, AAUP Executive Committee&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Shared Governance: Sen. Jeanine D&amp;rsquo;Armiento, Professor of Medicine in Anesthesiology and Chair of the Executive Committee of the University Senate, and Sen. Joseph Slaughter, Associate Professor of English and Comparative Literature&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This discussion will be introduced and moderated by:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Sen. Elizabeth Adeoye, Student, Columbia College and Co-Chair, External Relations and Research Policy Committee, and&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Sen. John Santelli, Professor of Population and Family Health and Pediatrics, School of Public Health, and Co-Chair, External Relations and Research Policy Committee.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Forum will first be held on March 26, from 5:15&amp;nbsp;to 7:15&amp;nbsp;pm in Jerome Greene Hall, Room 106 (Law School). The March 26 forum will be in-person only and open to current CUID holders. Registration is required.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Forum will be repeated on &lt;a href="http://events.columbia.edu/go/UniversityGovernance_Forum2" target="_blank"&gt;March 31, from 5:00 to 7:00 pm&amp;nbsp;in the Alumni Auditorium, Black Building&lt;/a&gt;, at the Columbia University Irving Medical Center from 5:00 to 7:00 pm on March 31. The March 31 forum will be open to current CUID holders and&amp;nbsp;alumni and will include a webinar option. Registration is required.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Questions may be submitted in advance to &lt;a href="mailto:senate@columbia.edu"&gt;senate@columbia.edu&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;or asked from the floor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Matthieu Verdeil, 2021, 80 min.&lt;br /&gt;
In French and English with English subtitles&lt;br /&gt;
Q&amp;amp;A with director Matthieu Verdeil and Brent Edwards&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A thrilling journey in the 1920s, from Marseille to Harlem, via Jamaica, Russia, and Morocco, in the footsteps of the Jamaican-American writer Claude McKay.&amp;nbsp; McKay was a rebellious figure of the Harlem Renaissance, a prominent literary movement of the 1920s &amp;ndash;&amp;nbsp; an unclassifiable author who wandered for more than 10 years in Europe, frequenting the artistic and political avant-gardes.&amp;nbsp; His work ranged from vernacular verse celebrating peasant life in Jamaica to poems that protested racial and economic inequities.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Watch the trailer &lt;a href="https://vimeo.com/606833247?fl=pl&amp;amp;fe=vl"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Join us for a movie:Water For Life which explores the collision of water rights, Indigenous beliefs, and resource extraction through the lives of three Latin American community leaders. The right to clean water is a global issue&amp;mdash;in Latin America it has become a matter of life and death.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Narrated by Mexican actor Diego Luna with the original song Ko (Water) sung by Grammy Award-winning Mexican Mixtec singer Lila Downs and Chilean Mapuche singer-songwriter Daniela Millaleo.&lt;/p&gt;
